{"groups":[{"members":["c1"],"primary":"c1","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law: 30-year exemption from local rent control","requirement":"New Jersey has no statewide rent cap, and municipalities may adopt rent control ordinances. Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction is completed.","key_value":"30-year exemption from local rent control after completion of construction","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings, for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","exemptions":"The same source also states that some housing is not subject to municipal rent control: government-subsidized rents, HUD-owned or HUD-subsidized housing, certain HUD-insured developments that HUD finds have economic problems, and NJHMFA-financed projects whose rents the NJHMFA fixes.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c2"],"primary":"c2","title":"Anti-Eviction Act: rent increases must not be unconscionable","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease and must end the old lease with written notice of the increased rent. Any increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal rent increase ordinance. Rent may not be raised to cover costs that result solely from conversion to a condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","key_value":"No numeric cap; increase must not be unconscionable","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1 et seq.; N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies covered by the Anti-Eviction Act.","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c2","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
